    part st123 : Statement {
        :>> id = "2e9c7c89-208a-4ea0-a4e5-27b4f1d2ef66";
        :>> title = "GH#74 [proposal] keel claim commits the claim file by pathspec (or land refuses a claim commit with ride-alongs) - a whole-index commit carried another session's staged deletion";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> createdBy = "githubRecorder";
        :>> text = "**Proposal.** Make a claim commit structurally unable to carry a ride-along: `keel claim` should commit exactly `.tracking/claims/<actor>.sysml` by pathspec (`git commit -- .tracking/claims/<actor>.sysml`), or `keel land` should refuse to push a claim commit whose tree touches anything outside `.tracking/claims/`.\n\n**What happened downstream.** Penumbra, two sessions sharing one checkout. Landing claim `claimai001` (commit `558ce25`) committed the deletion of `tools/spoke-augment-use-cases.py` alongside the claim file, because the deletion was already in the git index - staged by the co-resident session whose in-flight work replaced that spoke - and `git commit` takes the whole index. `main` then deleted the Python spoke while `README.md`, `CONTRIBUTORS.md` and `spokes/README.md` at HEAD still referenced it, ahead of the replacement landing. Recorded as Penumbra issue088.\n\n**Measured since (git log --stat over the claim-titled commits, 2026-09-14).** Five commits titled as claims: two touched only the claim file (`b4f9530`, `4ae15eb`); two carried tracking edits their messages name deliberately (`4cc0c02`, `5f21906`); one carried the unintended ride-along (`558ce25`). Manual staging discipline held four times in five, which is what a habit does; the control is the pathspec.\n\n**Where.** At v0.4.1, `keel claim` (`keel-cli/src/claim.rs`, dispatched at `keel-cli/src/main.rs` line 4814) writes the claim file and makes no commit; `keel land` (`keel-cli/src/sync.rs` line 205, `cmd_land`) gates the tree and pushes the branch. Neither knows which commit is the claim, so the whole-index commit is left to the operator.\n\n**Suggested shape.** Either (a) `keel claim` ends by committing the claim file by pathspec with a fixed message, so the operator never runs `git commit` for a claim; or (b) `cmd_land` inspects the commits it is about to push and refuses one whose message is the claim form and whose tree touches paths outside `.tracking/claims/`. (a) removes the hazard; (b) detects it. Either makes the record verifiable after the fact without a human remembering to stage nothing else.\n\nFiled from Penumbra, 2026-09-14 (proposeClaimPathspecCommit, resolving issue088).\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/sysmlv2-ai-toolkit/issues/74";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st124 : Statement {
        :>> id = "811ce417-801a-4fc7-be1c-2e419b103fe0";
        :>> title = "GH#73 [control defect] keel land --help does not print usage - it runs the landing and pushes the branch";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> createdBy = "githubRecorder";
        :>> text = "**Observed.** `keel land --help` did not print usage. It ran the landing and pushed the current branch:\n\n```\n$ keel land --help\nkeel land: pushing vintage-0.4.1-and-review-corpus -> origin (attempt 1/3)\nkeel land: landed.\n```\n\nkeel 0.4.1 (binary and pin agree). The branch had no upstream before; it now exists on the remote.\n\n**Where.** `keel-cli/src/main.rs` line 4774 dispatches `Some(''land'') => keel_cli::sync::cmd_land(&repo_arg(rest), 3)`, and `cmd_land` (`keel-cli/src/sync.rs` line 205) takes only the repository path: an unknown flag in `rest` is neither refused nor read. Other subcommands refuse the same probe by name - `keel validate --help` says ''`--help` looks like a flag, not a path'', `keel sync-claude --help` says ''unknown flag `--help`'', `keel record decision --help` prints the required arguments.\n\n**Why it matters.** `land` is the one keel command whose effect leaves the machine. A documentation probe that becomes a push is a control defect in the sense of D0047: the operator's intent was to read, the tool acted. Downstream (Penumbra) has a standing rule that the AI never pushes; the probe broke it without any write intent.\n\n**Suggested shape.** Give `land` the same argument discipline `validate` and `sync-claude` have: any argument beginning with `-` refuses with usage and exit 2, before `gate_passes` and before the push loop. A regression test in `keel-cli/tests/land_gate.rs`: `land --help` against a repository with a bare remote leaves the remote untouched and exits non-zero.\n\nFiled from Penumbra, 2026-09-14.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/sysmlv2-ai-toolkit/issues/73";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st125 : Statement {
        :>> id = "afde8da4-0bf6-413c-ac8b-35875e7fd042";
        :>> title = "GH#72 [bug] keel-parser refuses a NAMED dependency (dependency <name> from A to B;) - the OMG grammar allows Identification? before from";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> createdBy = "githubRecorder";
        :>> text = "**Observed.** `keel validate` refuses a SysML v2 file at the first *named* dependency:\n\n```\nFAIL:  ./.tracking/Shadow.sysml — ./.tracking/Shadow.sysml:798468:16: expected From, got Ident(''derive_50511'')\n```\n\nThe statement it refused:\n\n```\n    dependency derive_50511 from LOGDC2_PRD_Rqmts_105 to LOGDC2_SYSRQ_418;\n```\n\n**Grammar.** The OMG pilot implementation's textual grammar allows an optional identification before `from` (`org.omg.sysml.xtext/src/org/omg/sysml/xtext/SysML.xtext`, rule `Dependency`, lines 53-59):\n\n```\n'dependency' ( Identification? 'from' )?\n    client += [SysML::Element|QualifiedName] ( ',' client += ... )* 'to'\n    supplier += [SysML::Element|QualifiedName] ( ',' supplier += ... )*\n    RelationshipBody\n```\n\nSo `dependency <name> from A to B;` is vanilla SysML v2.\n\n**Where keel diverges.** `keel-parser/src/parser.rs`, `parse_bare_dependency` (v0.4.1, lines 738-741): after consuming `dependency` it does `p.expect(&TokenKind::From, filename)?` with no room for an identification, so any named dependency fails as above. The marker form (`#Marker dependency ...`, line 724-725) has the same shape.\n\n**Reproduction.** Penumbra (a downstream keel project that emits a Jama project as vanilla SysML v2) emitted its full shadow - 38,723 elements, 5,409 dependencies, 115 MB - and ran `keel validate` over it as an independent conformance parser:\n\n1. as emitted: refused at the first dependency (above);\n2. the same file with the names removed by `sed -E 's/^(    dependency) [A-Za-z0-9_]+ from //1 from /'`: `1 tracking file(s) validated clean.`\n\nThe two runs isolate the divergence to the identification alone; everything else in the emitted text parses.\n\n**Why it matters downstream.** The name carries the source relationship id, which the emitter needs for round-tripping edges (`dependency derive_50511` is Jama relationship 50511). Dropping the name would lose the id; keeping it means keel cannot be used as the independent conformance check until the grammar's optional identification is read.\n\n**Suggested shape.** In both dependency parsers, after `dependency`, accept an optional `Ident` (and `<shortName>`) before `From`, and carry it on `DependencyAnnotation` (a `name: Option<String>`) so the census can count named and unnamed alike.\n\nFiled from Penumbra, 2026-09-14 (sprint 199, retroIndependentParserForConformance).\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/sysmlv2-ai-toolkit/issues/72";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st126 : Statement {
        :>> id = "23296527-7207-40df-b035-9639f93c8c49";
        :>> title = "split keel into granular workspace members; keel-issues, not an external tracker";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "totally agree on splitting the crate into workspace members.  give us lots of granularity - no need to couple code just to keep it in one place.  making it broad and uncoupled makes it modular and easy to rebuild.  i like the ''test binaries'' improvements and the Guards one also.  I think your identification of ''where the boundary sits'' is a bit naive.  What we're talking about here is the value of granularly splitting apart keel into disparate functions, which is a great idea and i expect will largely help the issues with recompiling, etc.  But the second implicit idea here is - use an issue tracker to enforce process and track/expose state.  I think that's fine, but I do think Keel does it better in many ways - we can read plain sysml processes, interpret them, add guards (really jsut CI/CD), etc.  This has become a more and more obvious need as this project goes forward - how to hook into custom processes that have absolute discipline?  You're proposing an issue tracker, which is fine.  I say let's just make keel-issues or something, which can manage the subset of processes related to items/issues (and not e.g. decisions about keel itself).  Big modularity pass incoming, I think.  Decouple the code wherever it's helpful, and let's divide and conquer to increase speed, reliability, and anchor points into our processes where possible.";
        :>> saidBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-14"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::chat;
    }
}
